<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amaziah of Judah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">early 8th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-david">House of David</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amaziah was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> in the early eighth century BCE, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joash-of-judah">Joash of Judah</a>. According to the Books of Kings he defeated the Edomites but then challenged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehoash-of-israel">Jehoash of Israel</a>, who defeated and captured him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beth-shemesh">Beth-shemesh</a>, broke down part of the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and plundered the Temple. He was later murdered in a conspiracy at Lachish.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amaziah-of-judah.jpg" alt="Amaziah of Judah" width="320" height="321" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Guillaume Rouille · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amasias.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Amaziah was the ninth king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>, the son and successor of Joash. His mother was Jehoaddan and his son was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzziah">Uzziah</a>. He took the throne at the age of 25, after the assassination of his father, and reigned for 29 years, according to 2 Kings and 2 Chronicles. Edwin R. Thiele dates his reign to 797/796 to 768/767 BCE, with Uzziah becoming co-regent in the fifth year of the reign. The books of Kings and Chronicles consider him a righteous king, but with some hesitation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>As soon as his kingdom was established, Amaziah executed his father's murderers but let their children live, in obedience to the Mosaic law that a person shall be put to death for his own sin. According to Chronicles he hired a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> army of 100,000 soldiers from Ephraim for 100 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a>, to supplement 300,000 Judean soldiers, in an attempt to reconquer Edom, which had rebelled in the reign of his great-grandfather Jehoram. An unnamed prophet commanded him to send the mercenaries back, and he did so, followed by a decisive victory over the Edomites. The angry mercenaries attacked and looted several towns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>.</p><p>After the victory Chronicles says Amaziah brought the idol of the conquered Edomites to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, and when a prophet rebuked him he threatened him with execution. His pride swollen, he challenged Jehoash, king of Israel and grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehu">Jehu</a>, to recognise his suzerainty or fight. Jehoash answered with the parable of the thistle and the cedar. Amaziah refused to be warned and rushed into a disastrous battle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beth-shemesh">Beth-shemesh</a>. He was captured, 400 cubits of the wall of Jerusalem were broken down, the city, the Temple and the palace were looted, and hostages were taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>A conspiracy followed in which he was killed. Tradition holds that he was tortured to death by his own subjects at Lachish, where he had fled, and his body was brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and buried in the royal sepulchre. Kings records the conspiracy simply, while Chronicles connects it with his turning away from the Lord.</p><p>The Books of Kings say he did what was right in the sight of the Lord but fell short of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>'s standard, because he did not remove the high places and centralise worship in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. Chronicles says that he did right but not with a loyal heart. The rabbis of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talmud">Talmud</a> held, on a tradition, that the prophet Amoz was his brother, which would make <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaiah">Isaiah</a> a member of the royal family.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Amaziah of Judah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Early wars were fought against the Kingdom of Israel, with which Judah alternated between conflict and alliance; Jehoshaphat fought beside Ahab of Israel, but <b>Amaziah</b> was defeated by Jehoash of Israel, who broke down part of the wall of Jerusalem.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-judah">Kingdom of Judah · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Jerusalem, in what is now <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a>. Died in Lachish, in what is now <a href="/countries/israel">Israel</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.78,35.23&amp;year=-735" aria-label="Jerusalem on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Amaziah of Judah"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5380 4839h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5380 4839h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.78,35.23&amp;year=-735">What was here in 735 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
